NNAAMMEE
    roffsub - check, compile, and render
    terminal manuals written in roffsub

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
    rrooffffssuubb [--ww _w_i_d_t_h] [--ss _s_t_y_l_e] [--mm _m_e_a_s_u_r_e] [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --ee [--ss _s_t_y_l_e] [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --cc _f_i_l_e ...
    rrooffffssuubb --ii [--dd _d_a_t_e] [--rr _r_e_v] [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --pp [--ww _w_i_d_t_h] [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --gg cc|bbaasshh _n_a_m_e [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --nn [--dd _d_a_t_e] [--rr _r_e_v] [_f_i_l_e]
    rrooffffssuubb --hh | ----hheellpp | --VV

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
    rrooffffssuubb works with manuals written
    in roffsub, a subset of the man
    macros and tbl that every roff
    program displays and that small awk
    and C routines can also render. It
    renders a manual for the terminal in
    use, at that terminal's width, with
    bold and italic where the terminal
    can show them. It also checks a
    manual's form, compiles it into
    records, formats it at a fixed
    width, and writes the text as a C
    array or a bash heredoc for a
    program to carry.

    rrooffffssuubb comes as a C binary and as a
    bash function that behave alike. The
    function serves systems where
    programs cannot be run from the
    user's own directories, such as a
    home directory mounted noexec, so
    one package works everywhere. Both
    use awk to check, compile, and
    generate; the binary renders records
    and pre-formatted text without it.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
    --ww _w_i_d_t_h
        Render at _w_i_d_t_h columns. Without
        it the width comes from
        MMAANNWWIIDDTTHH, then from the
        terminal, then 80; output that
        is not a terminal is 80 columns
        unless MMAANNWWIIDDTTHH is set.

    --ss _s_t_y_l_e
        Write emphasis as ppllaaiinn (none),
        oovveerrssttrriikkee (backspace sequences
        for a pager), ssggrr (terminal
        control sequences), or pprree (font
        escapes for later emission).
        Without it the style follows the
        destination; see OOUUTTPPUUTT CCHHOOIICCEESS.

    --mm _m_e_a_s_u_r_e
        Never fill text past _m_e_a_s_u_r_e
        columns, however wide the
        terminal.

    --ee  Emit pre-formatted text, as
        written by --pp, in the chosen
        style.

    --cc  Check the form of each _f_i_l_e and
        report every departure from
        roffsub as _f_i_l_e:_l_i_n_e: _i_d:
        _m_e_s_s_a_g_e. The exit status is 1
        when an error was found. With
        RROOFFFFSSUUBB__SSTTRRIICCTT set, warnings
        count as errors too.

    --ii  Write the compiled records of
        _f_i_l_e. --dd and --rr fill in @@DDAATTEE@@
        and @@VVEERRSSIIOONN@@ on the ..TTHH line.

    --pp  Write _f_i_l_e formatted at a fixed
        width (default 80) with its font
        changes kept, for a program's
        pprreeffoorrmmaatttteedd profile.

    --gg cc|bbaasshh _n_a_m_e
        Write the lines of _f_i_l_e as a C
        array called _n_a_m_e, or as a
        marked bash region that reads
        them into the variable _n_a_m_e.

    --nn  Write _f_i_l_e as the page to
        install for man. --dd and --rr fill
        in the ..TTHH line as for --ii, and
        each table is rewritten so that
        roff draws it as roffsub does:
        no vertical lines, and a + where
        a rule meets a column gap. The
        source itself stays as written.

    --hh  Print the usage summary.

    ----hheellpp
        Show this manual.

    --VV  Print the version.

    A _f_i_l_e may be a roffsub source or
    its compiled records; records begin
    with the line vv, a tab, and 11.
    Without a _f_i_l_e the standard input is
    read.

OOUUTTPPUUTT CCHHOOIICCEESS
    OOUUTTPPUUTT: terminal
    GGOOEESS TTOO: the pager
    SSTTYYLLEE: overstrike
    WWIIDDTTHH: terminal

    OOUUTTPPUUTT: terminal, PAGER is cat
    GGOOEESS TTOO: the terminal
    SSTTYYLLEE: sgr
    WWIIDDTTHH: terminal

    OOUUTTPPUUTT: file or pipe
    GGOOEESS TTOO: the file or pipe
    SSTTYYLLEE: plain
    WWIIDDTTHH: 80

    The pager is PPAAGGEERR when set,
    otherwise lleessss when installed,
    otherwise mmoorree. TTEERRMM==dduummbb selects
    plain. Command-line options override
    the environment, and the environment
    overrides these defaults.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
    MMAANNWWIIDDTTHH
        The rendering width.

    CCOOLLUUMMNNSS
        The terminal width, when
        MMAANNWWIIDDTTHH is not set.

    PPAAGGEERR
        The pager for terminal output.

    RROOFFFFSSUUBB__SSTTYYLLEE
        The style: plain, overstrike,
        sgr, or pre.

    RROOFFFFSSUUBB__SSGGRR
        Control sequence parameters for
        bold and italic, as
        bb==11;;3333::ii==44;;3366. The defaults are
        1 (bold) and 4 (underline).
        Colors let bold and italic show
        as distinct colors.

    RROOFFFFSSUUBB__MMEEAASSUURREE
        The maximum line length.

    RROOFFFFSSUUBB__HHEEAADDIINNGG
        The number of columns section
        headings are moved in from the
        margin; 0 by default. Everything
        else stays in place.

    RROOFFFFSSUUBB__SSTTRRIICCTT
        When set, the check treats
        warnings as errors.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
    0 on success, 1 when a check found
    errors, 2 on a usage or input error.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
    Check a manual, then read it as a
    program would show it:

    roffsub -c srt.1
    roffsub srt.1
    roffsub -w 50 -s plain srt.1 > srt.txt

    Embed the full manual in a C program
    and in a bash function:

    roffsub -i -d 2026-11-15 -r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.ir
    roffsub -g c srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.h
    roffsub -g bash _srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.bash

    Write the page to install:

    roffsub -n -d 2026-11-15 -r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.1.out

    Embed a pre-formatted manual in a
    small program:

    roffsub -p srt.1 > srt.pre
    roffsub -g c srt_man srt.pre > srt.man.h

